# Question: how long does an item take from start to finish, given the work in
# progress and the throughput, and how likely is it to take at most two weeks
# once work in progress is limited?
#
# Run it (it uses the shared formulas):
#   ubc --scenario delivery/littles-law
# Change an assumption, for example a stricter limit:
#   ubc --set 'wip_limit = 5' --scenario delivery/littles-law
# See which assumption matters most:
#   ubc --sens lead_limited --scenario delivery/littles-law
#
# Every "a to b" is a 90% confidence interval. Little's law holds for averages
# in a stable system: limiting work in progress does not make the team finish
# more items a week, it makes each item wait less.
#
# Sources: John D. C. Little, "A Proof for the Queuing Formula L = λW"
# (1961), for Little's law; the ranges are examples, take them from your board.

# Assumptions
wip = 15 to 30                    # items started and not finished; count the cards in progress on your board
throughput = 4 to 7               # items finished a week; take it from the last weeks in your tracker
wip_limit = 8                     # the limit the team would agree on; your team's choice
target = 2                        # weeks the business would like an item to take; ask your stakeholders

# Calculation
lead = lead_time(wip, throughput)                 # weeks an item takes today
lead_limited = lead_time(wip_limit, throughput)   # weeks it would take with the limit
weeks_saved = lead - lead_limited                 # what the limit takes off every item

# Result
lead
lead_limited
weeks_saved
P(lead_limited <= target)         # how likely is an item to take at most two weeks with the limit?
